  "image_name": "483b0072a812d192bb1ef25deeb741df.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a membrane-based seawater electrolyser designed to use intermittent renewable electricity, illustrated by wind turbines and a solar panel feeding the device. Seawater, shown as a mixed electrolyte containing ions such as sodium, chloride, magnesium, and calcium, enters the electrolyser stack. At the core is a membrane that appears to preferentially transport hydroxide ions while blocking many other dissolved ions. The listed membrane advantages emphasize high selectivity and conductivity, along with chemical, mechanical, and anti-fouling stability.\n\nThe central exploded view shows the electrolyser architecture, including bipolar plates, gas diffusion layers, catalyst layers, a gasket, and the membrane between anode and cathode. The lower schematics highlight the key interface problems and protections on each side: at the anode, chloride-related reactions and corrosion are important concerns, while at the cathode magnesium and calcium can deposit as precipitates and chloride can also contribute to corrosion. The figure indicates strategies for chloride inhibition, metal-precipitate inhibition, and corrosion resistance at these electrodes.\n\nOverall, the message is that a selective membrane and tailored electrode design enable seawater electrolysis powered by variable renewables for uses such as transportation, chemical synthesis, and metal refining.",
